Consider a fleet of logging trucks that transport timber from harvesting sites (loading points) to mills or terminals (unloading points). Each loading and unloading point has a limited number of servers (e.g., loaders, cranes, weigh stations) so that arriving trucks may need to wait in a queue. The state of the system evolves stochastically in real time: travel times depend on weather and road conditions, queues build up dynamically, trucks may break down, and new transportation requests or cancellations may appear during the day. Trucks, loaders, and a central dispatch unit can exchange information continuously.

Develop a vehicle routing and dispatching framework for this setting. Concretely, formulate a mathematical model of the forestry vehicle routing problem (VRP) that:

1. Treats truck-to-trip assignments and route choices as decisions that can be re-optimized at any decision epoch on a rolling horizon;
2. Explicitly represents queues and service times at loading and unloading points (e.g., through a queueing-network component or time-dependent capacity constraints);
3. Incorporates stochastic and time-varying inputs such as travel-time disturbances due to weather, breakdowns of trucks, and updates of supply and demand information.

Design solution algorithms (exact, heuristic, metaheuristic, or learning-based) that, given the current system state, produce updated routing and dispatching decisions fast enough for online use, and analyze their performance (e.g., cost, waiting time, throughput, robustness) against static or myopic policies.

